WEB OBSERVATION – OPTIONS 1, 2 & 3
====================================================

Autono-Me can observe the live browser state before acting.
Use these when you are unsure of the correct selector or the page is dynamic.

====================================================
NEW ACTIONS
====================================================

ACTION: snapshot
Takes a full page observation combining:
- Option 1: Accessibility tree (all roles, names, states)
- Option 2: Screenshot (AI sees the page visually)
- Option 3: CDP element map (every interactive element with bounding boxes)

Use snapshot BEFORE a click or fill you are unsure about.
The AI uses the observation to find the correct selector automatically.

{
  "action": "snapshot"
}

ACTION: cdplocate
Lighter than snapshot — just the interactive element map.
Returns: role | text | selector | x y w h for every button/input/link/select.

{
  "action": "cdplocate"
}

FLAG: observeBefore + description
Add to ANY click, fill, hover, or press step.
Before executing the step, the system takes a full page observation
and asks the AI to find the best selector for the described element.
Use this instead of guessing selectors for dynamic pages.

{
  "action": "click",
  "description": "the first video thumbnail in search results",
  "observeBefore": true
}

{
  "action": "fill",
  "description": "the main search input box",
  "observeBefore": true,
  "value": "cats"
}

====================================================
WHEN TO USE OBSERVATION
====================================================

USE observeBefore: true WHEN:
- The site is a SPA (React, Vue, Angular) with dynamic content
- You are not certain of the CSS selector
- Previous attempts with a selector failed
- The page may have loaded differently than expected
- Interacting with search boxes, video thumbnails, menus, dropdowns

USE snapshot WHEN:
- You want to observe the page once and plan multiple steps based on what you find
- You need to understand the page structure before writing the workflow

ALWAYS use observeBefore for YouTube video clicks:
